\begin{abstract}
Let $P_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\langle\nabla\alpha,\nabla\cdot\rangle$ be a Witten operator on a closed connected Riemannian manifold, acting in $L^2(e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g)$. The spectrum of $P_\alpha$ does not determine $(g,\alpha)$. The full multiplication table of eigenfunctions does. This paper isolates a finite piece of that table.

Fix cutoffs $\Lambda\le\Lambda'<\infty$ and let $\mathcal{V}$ be the sum of eigenspaces with eigenvalue at most $\Lambda$. The visible packet of the window is the restricted triple products of pairs from $\mathcal{V}$ against modes up to $\Lambda'$, together with the $L^2$ tail $\varepsilon$ of those products and the weighted-tail constant $K$ of Wyman type. No Weyl law, no potential, and no curvature bound is used.

If $\varepsilon=0$, the packet determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to a $C^\infty$ isometry of $g$ and an additive constant on $\alpha$. If $\varepsilon$ is smaller than a threshold depending on the given pair, it determines them up to a $C^k$ almost-isometry of defect $O(K\varepsilon)$. The manifold is recovered as the space of almost-characters of the window, the metric by dualizing the cometric of $P$ on $\mathcal{V}$, and the weight from the ratio of the $L^2$ measure to Riemannian volume.

The unweighted first window on the sphere is the minimal case: $\Lambda=n$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, and $\varepsilon=0$ already at $\Lambda'=2n+2$. That frame recognizes the unit round sphere exactly, and recognizes it with linear $C^0$ stability when the same frame is allowed small deficits. Those statements are frame-side. They are not Obata theorems.

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} sphere, spectral window, triple products, Witten Operators, eigenfunctions, inverse problem
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
A Witten operator on a closed Riemannian manifold $(X,g)$ is the weighted Laplacian
$$P_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\langle\nabla\alpha,\nabla\cdot\rangle$$
acting in $L^2(e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g)$. Its spectrum is discrete, nonnegative, and unbounded, with kernel the constant functions. The geometric data of the pair are the metric $g$ and the weight $\alpha$; equivalently, the Riemannian volume and the measure $\mu=e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g$. Ordinary Laplace–Beltrami operators are the case $\alpha=0$.

The spectrum alone does not determine $(g,\alpha)$. What does determine them, in the large, is the multiplication table of eigenfunctions
$$\Phi(u,v,w)=\int_X uvw\,d\mu$$
together with the list of eigenvalues. That is a global inverse-spectral statement: it consumes every eigenspace. The purpose of this article is a finite substitute. We fix two cutoffs $\Lambda\le\Lambda'<\infty$ and let
$$\mathcal{V}=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda}\ker(P-\lambda)$$
be the corresponding spectral window. Products of window functions need not lie in $\mathcal{V}$. We compare them to the slightly larger window $\mathcal{V}'$ of modes up to $\Lambda'$, and we write
$$\varepsilon=\sup_{\lVert u\rVert_2=\lVert v\rVert_2=1}\bigl\lVert(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)\bigr\rVert_{L^2(\mu)}$$
for the uniform tail of those products. For fixed $\Lambda$ one has $\varepsilon\to 0$ as $\Lambda'\to\infty$; the theory uses one finite pair $(\Lambda,\Lambda')$, not that limit. The exact subclass is $\varepsilon=0$ at a finite $\Lambda'$, which is an algebraic constraint on the window, not an asymptotic one.

The visible packet of the window is
$$\bigl(\Lambda,\ \Lambda',\ \sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda'],\ \Phi\big|_{\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'},\ \varepsilon,\ K\bigr),$$
where $K$ is the constant in the weighted tail bound
$$\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda^2\bigl\lvert\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\bigr\rvert^2
\le K^2\,\varepsilon(u,v)^2\,\lVert u\rVert_2^2\lVert v\rVert_2^2.$$

The cometric $\Gamma$ of $g$ is not an extra input. The Witten multiplication rule
$$P(uv)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-2\Gamma(u,v)$$
computes $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ from $(P,\Phi)$, with an $L^2$ remainder at most $K\varepsilon$. Two nondegeneracy hypotheses close the packet: evaluation $X\to\mathcal{V}^\ast$ is a smooth embedding, and $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ spans $T^*X$. No Weyl law is assumed, no potential is present, and no curvature bound is used.

The main recognition theorem is that this packet determines $(X,g,\alpha)$. If $\varepsilon=0$, it determines them up to a $C^\infty$ Riemannian isometry of $g$ and an additive constant on $\alpha$. If $\varepsilon$ is smaller than a threshold depending on $(P,\Lambda,\Lambda')$, it determines them up to a $C^k$ almost-isometry of defect $O(K\varepsilon)$. In both regimes the underlying manifold is recovered as the space of (almost) multiplicative characters of the window, the metric is recovered by dualizing $\Gamma$, and the weight is recovered as the logarithm of the ratio of $\mu$ to Riemannian volume.

The class of pairs to which the theorem applies is large. Every closed Witten operator, including every Laplace–Beltrami operator, admits some finite embedding window; the packet is then defined, and reconstruction proceeds. The theorem is recognition, not construction: $P$ is given as Witten, and the packet is asked to recover the geometry that produced it. It is also not a spectral uniqueness theorem. Two isospectral Witten operators with different window products need not be isometric.

A distinguished slice is the unweighted sphere. There one may take $\Lambda=n$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, and $\varepsilon=0$ already at $\Lambda'=2n+2$: products of linear coordinates on $S^n$ occupy only the constant slot and the quadratic harmonics. Theorems 1 and 2 then specialize to an exact recognition of the unit round sphere from that frame, and to a linear $C^0$ stability statement when the same frame is allowed small defects. Those two statements are compared with Obata’s theorem and with quantitative Obata theorems on the Ricci side; they are not Ricci theorems. They use a linear frame and its products, not a lower bound on Ricci curvature and a single first eigenfunction.

The analytic ingredients are elementary. The weighted tail bound is the one place where elliptic estimates enter, and they enter as a constant $K(P,\Lambda,\Lambda')$, not as a curvature comparison. Wyman’s bound on window triples supplies the polynomial control on $\Phi$. The weighted tail constant $K$ is the axiom W1$^+$ on the ratio $\lVert P(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)\rVert_2/\varepsilon(u,v)$. Sobolev estimates bound the numerator of that ratio, not automatically the ratio itself. Neither bound is a curvature comparison.

The article is organized as follows. Section 2 fixes the Witten category and the window packet. Section 3 extracts $\Gamma$ from the multiplication rule and records the constant $K$. Section 4 identifies points of $X$ with characters of the window and promotes a small tail to a $C^1$ persistence of that identification. Section 5 dualizes $\Gamma$ to a metric and reads $\alpha$ from the two measures. Section 6 states the recognition theorem, exact and approximate, with the isometry claim separated by the size of $\varepsilon$. Section 7 specializes to the unweighted first window on the sphere. Section 8 collects what the packet does not determine.

Throughout, the governing principle is the same as in the global inverse problem: geometry sits in the multiplication table, not in the list of eigenvalues. The contribution here is that a finite, explicitly tailed piece of that table already reconstructs a Witten pair, and that the unit sphere is the minimal unweighted instance of the same mechanism.

\subsection{Results}
Standing hypotheses for all four statements: $X$ closed and connected, $P=P_\alpha$ a Witten operator, packet as in §2, $u,v\in\mathcal{V}_\Lambda$, Wyman on window triples, W1$^+$, W2, W4.
\begin{theorem}[exact recognition]
\label{thm-1}
Assume $\varepsilon=0$. The packet
$$\bigl(\Lambda,\Lambda',\sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda'],\Phi\big|_{\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'}\bigr)$$
determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to a $C^\infty$ Riemannian isometry of $g$ and an additive constant on $\alpha$. Evaluation is a Riemannian isometry of $(X,g)$ onto the character space of $(\mathcal{V},m)$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$, and $P$ is the Witten operator of the reconstructed pair.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[approximate recognition]
\label{thm-2}
There exist $\varepsilon_*=\varepsilon_*(P,\Lambda,\Lambda')>0$ and constants $C(P,\Lambda,\Lambda',k)$ such that if $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_*$, the same packet determines a $C^\infty$ submanifold $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$ and a metric $g_\Sigma$ for which evaluation $\iota:X\to\Sigma$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism satisfying
$$\bigl\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
+\bigl\lVert\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
\le C\,K\,\varepsilon.$$
In particular $\iota$ is a $C^k$ almost-isometry of defect $O(K\varepsilon)$, not an isometry.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[round sphere, exact window]
\label{thm-a}
Assume $\alpha=0$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, $\Lambda=n$, and $\varepsilon=0$ at $\Lambda'=2n+2$. Suppose there is a frame $x_0,\dots,x_n$ in $\mathcal{V}$ with
$$Px_a=nx_a,\quad
\int x_ax_b=\delta_{ab},\quad
\int x_a=0,\quad
\sum_ax_a^2=1,$$
each $x_ax_b-\delta_{ab}/(n+1)$ an eigenfunction at $2n+2$, and $\{dx_a\}$ spanning $T^*X$. Then $X$ is isometric to the unit round sphere, $P=-\Delta$, and $\operatorname{span}\{x_a\}=H_1$. Scaling: the pair $(\lambda,2\lambda+2\lambda/n)$ gives radius $\sqrt{n/\lambda}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[round sphere, quantitative window]
In the setting of Theorem~\ref{thm-a}, allow deficits
$$\delta_1=\Bigl\lVert\sum_ax_a^2-1\Bigr\rVert_{C^0},\quad
\delta_2=\bigl\lVert(I-\Pi_0-\Pi_{2n+2})(x_ax_b)\bigr\rVert_{L^2},$$
$$\delta_3=\text{rank gap of }\Gamma(x_a,x_b),\quad
\delta_\lambda=\lvert\lambda-n\rvert,$$
and set $\delta=\delta_1+\delta_2+\delta_3+\delta_\lambda+\varepsilon$. There exist $\delta_*=\delta_*(n)>0$ and $C(n)$ such that if $\delta<\delta_*$, then $X$ is a $C^1$-graph over $S^n$ and
$$d_{\mathrm{GH}}(X,S^n)+d_{C^0}(g,g_{\mathrm{rd}})\le C(n)\,\delta.$$
\end{theorem}

This is linear stability of Theorem~\ref{thm-a}. It does not use Ricci curvature.

\section{The Packet}
Let $X$ be a closed connected smooth manifold and let
$$P=P_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\langle\nabla\alpha,\nabla\cdot\rangle$$
act in $L^2(X,\mu)$ with $d\mu=e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g$. Assume $P$ is essentially self-adjoint, with discrete spectrum
$$0=\lambda_0<\lambda_1\le\lambda_2\le\cdots\to+\infty$$
and $\ker P=\operatorname{span}\{1\}$. The principal symbol of $P$ is the cometric $\Gamma$ of $g$. For all smooth $u,v$,

\begin{equation}
P(uv)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-2\Gamma(u,v).\tag{A1}
\end{equation}

Fix cutoffs $\Lambda\le\Lambda'<\infty$. Write
$$\mathcal{V}=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda}\ker(P-\lambda),\qquad
\mathcal{V}'=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda'}\ker(P-\lambda),$$
and let $\Pi_{\Lambda'}$ be the orthogonal projection onto $\mathcal{V}'$. Throughout this section, $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$. Set
$$m(u,v)=\Pi_{\Lambda'}(uv),\qquad
\varepsilon(u,v)=\bigl\lVert(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)\bigr\rVert_{L^2(\mu)},$$
and
$$\varepsilon=\varepsilon(\Lambda,\Lambda')
=\sup_{\lVert u\rVert_2=\lVert v\rVert_2=1}\varepsilon(u,v).$$
At fixed $\Lambda$ one has $\varepsilon(\Lambda,\Lambda')\to 0$ as $\Lambda'\to\infty$. The article uses one finite pair $(\Lambda,\Lambda')$. The exact subclass is $\varepsilon=0$.
Write $\Phi(u,v,w)=\int_X uvw\,d\mu$.
Wyman. There exist $\delta\ge 0$ and $C<\infty$ such that, for all $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$ and every eigenfunction $w$,
$$\bigl\lvert\Phi(u,v,w)\bigr\rvert
\le C\,(1+\lambda_u+\lambda_v+\lambda_w)^\delta\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2\lVert w\rVert_2.$$
W1$^+$. There exists $K=K(P,\Lambda,\Lambda')<\infty$ such that for all $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$,
$$\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda^2\bigl\lvert\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\bigr\rvert^2
\le K^2\,\varepsilon(u,v)^2\,\lVert u\rVert_2^2\lVert v\rVert_2^2.$$
Equivalently,
$$\bigl\lVert P(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)\bigr\rVert_2
\le K\,\varepsilon(u,v)\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2.$$
Sobolev estimates bound the numerator; they do not automatically bound the ratio $K$.
W2. Evaluation $\iota:X\to\mathcal{V}^\ast$, $\iota(x)(u)=u(x)$, is a smooth embedding onto a closed submanifold of $\mathcal{V}^\ast$.
W4. The differentials $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ span $T^*X$ at every point.
The visible packet is
$$\bigl(\Lambda,\ \Lambda',\ \sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda'],\ \Phi\big|_{\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'},\ \varepsilon,\ K\bigr).$$
On pairs from $\mathcal{V}$, (A1) gives
$$2\Gamma(u,v)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-P\bigl(m(u,v)\bigr)+R(u,v),$$
with $\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_2\le K\,\varepsilon(u,v)\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2$. Thus $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ is not an independent input.

\section{The Cometric From the Window}

\begin{lemma} Let $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$. Then
$$2\Gamma(u,v)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-P\bigl(m(u,v)\bigr)+R(u,v),$$
where $R=-P(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)$ and
$$\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_{L^2(\mu)}
\le K\,\varepsilon(u,v)\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2.$$
If $\varepsilon=0$, then $R=0$ pointwise and $\Gamma(u,v)$ is determined by $(P,\Phi)$. The bilinear map $\Gamma:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\to C^\infty(X)$ does not depend on the orthonormal basis used to expand $\Phi$.
\label{lma-1}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Identity (A1) gives
$$2\Gamma(u,v)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-P(uv).$$
Split $uv=m(u,v)+t$ with $t=(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)$, so $\lVert t\rVert_2=\varepsilon(u,v)$. Then
$$P(uv)=P\bigl(m(u,v)\bigr)+Pt,$$
and $R=-Pt$. W1$^+$ is precisely
$$\lVert Pt\rVert_2\le K\,\varepsilon(u,v)\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2.$$
If $\varepsilon=0$, then $t=0$, hence $R=0$. In that case $m(u,v)=uv$ and every coefficient of $uv$ against $\mathcal{V}'$ is a value of $\Phi$, so the right-hand side is a function of the packet.
Basis-independence: $P$ and $\Phi$ are defined on $\mathcal{V}$ without choices, and $m$ is orthogonal projection of the pointwise product. Changing orthonormal bases of $\mathcal{V}$ and $\mathcal{V}'$ conjugates the array of $\Phi$-coefficients and leaves $\Gamma(u,v)$ unchanged.\
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}The constant $K$ is part of the packet. It depends on $(P,\Lambda,\Lambda')$ and need not be monotone in $\Lambda'$. An upper bound on the numerator $\lVert Pt\rVert_2$ follows from the Sobolev algebra estimate
$$\lVert uv\rVert_{H^s(\mu)}\le C_{\mathrm{alg}}(s)\,(1+\Lambda)^s\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2
\qquad(s>\max\{2,n/2\}),$$
together with $\lVert Pt\rVert_2\le C_{\mathrm{ell}}(P)\lVert t\rVert_{H^2}$. That bounds $\lVert Pt\rVert_2$, not the ratio $\lVert Pt\rVert_2/\varepsilon(u,v)$. The ratio is W1$^+$, not a Sobolev corollary.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[curvature and $K$]
The theorems do not use curvature. If one adds a bound on the geometry that produces $P$, the ratio $K$ becomes explicit.
On a closed Witten pair with
$$\operatorname{Ric}_g+\nabla^2\alpha\ge\kappa g,\qquad
\operatorname{inj}(g)\ge i_0,\qquad
\lVert(g,\alpha)\rVert_{C^s}\le M,$$
the Sobolev algebra constants and the elliptic constant for $P:H^2\to L^2$ are controlled by $(n,\kappa,i_0,M)$. Products of modes $\le\Lambda$ then satisfy
$$\lVert P(uv)\rVert_2\le C(n,\kappa,i_0,M,\Lambda)\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2.$$
If in addition $\sigma(P)$ has a gap $(\Lambda',\Lambda'+G]$, the tail Rayleigh quotient obeys
$$\Lambda'+G\le K\le C(n,\kappa,i_0,M,\Lambda,\Lambda').$$
In that regime $\Delta=K\varepsilon$ is a product of a geometrically bounded factor and the packet tail. The dependence of Theorem 2 on the unknown pair $(P,\Lambda,\Lambda')$ reduces to dependence on $(n,\kappa,i_0,M,\Lambda,\Lambda')$.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary} If $\varepsilon=0$, the Gram functions $\Gamma(u,v)$ for $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$ are packet data. W4 is then a nondegeneracy condition on those functions. If $\varepsilon>0$, the same Gram functions are determined up to an $L^2$ error of size $K\varepsilon$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Characters}
Let $m:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\to\mathcal{V}'$ be the packet product of §2. A linear form $\xi\in\mathcal{V}^\ast$ with $\xi(1)=1$ is an almost-character of tolerance $\varepsilon$ if
$$\bigl\lvert\xi\bigl(m(u,v)\bigr)-\xi(u)\xi(v)\bigr\rvert
\le C_0\varepsilon\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2
\qquad\text{for all }u,v\in\mathcal{V}.$$
Write $\mathcal{M}_\varepsilon$ for the set of such forms. Evaluations $\iota(x)$ lie in $\mathcal{M}_\varepsilon$: $\iota(x)(uv)=\iota(x)(u)\iota(x)(v)$ and the tail $t=(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)$ contributes at most $C\varepsilon$ after the $C^0$ embedding of the finite-dimensional space $\mathcal{V}'$.

\begin{lemma} There exist $\varepsilon_*=\varepsilon_*(P,\Lambda,\Lambda')>0$ and $C$ such that if $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_*$, then:
\label{lma-2}
$\iota(X)\subset\mathcal{M}_\varepsilon$;
every $\xi\in\mathcal{M}_\varepsilon$ lies within $C\varepsilon$ of $\iota(x)$ for a unique $x\in X$, in the dual Hilbert norm on $\mathcal{V}^\ast$;
the resulting map $\mathcal{M}_\varepsilon\to X$ is a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism if $\varepsilon=0$, and a $C^1$ diffeomorphism if $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_*$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} W2 says $\iota:X\to\mathcal{V}^\ast$ is a smooth embedding onto a closed submanifold. Consider
$$F(\xi;u,v)=\xi\bigl(m(u,v)\bigr)-\xi(u)\xi(v)$$
as a $C^\infty$ section, in $\xi$, of a finite-dimensional bundle of bilinear forms on $\mathcal{V}$. At $\varepsilon=0$, $F(\,\cdot\,;u,v)$ vanishes exactly on $\iota(X)$. Indeed the algebra generated by $\mathcal{V}$ under the exact product $m$ separates points (W2) and is unital, so its characters are the evaluations; Stone–Weierstrass in the compact-open topology upgrades that to $C(X)$.
The zero set is transverse at $\varepsilon=0$. Differentiating $F$ in the $\mathcal{V}^\ast$-directions at $\xi=\iota(x)$ yields the family
$$(u,v)\mapsto \xi(u)\,dv+\xi(v)\,du$$
on $T^*_xX$. W4 says $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ spans $T^*_xX$, so this family is surjective onto the annihilator of $T_{\iota(x)}\iota(X)$. Persistence of transverse zeros gives, for $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_*$, a unique $C^1$ section $\xi\mapsto x(\xi)$ of a tubular neighborhood of $\iota(X)$, with
$$\lVert\xi-\iota\bigl(x(\xi)\bigr)\rVert_{\mathcal{V}^\ast}\le C\varepsilon.$$
Compactness of $X$ makes the local section global and single-valued. At $\varepsilon=0$ the section is $\iota^{-1}$ and is $C^\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary} If $\varepsilon=0$, $X$ is identified with the character space of the finite algebra $(\mathcal{V},m)$ as a $C^\infty$ embedded submanifold $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$. If $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_*$, the same identification holds up to a $C^1$ diffeomorphism of defect $O(\varepsilon)$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The constant $\varepsilon_*$ depends on the given pair $(P,\Lambda,\Lambda')$, through the $C^1$ geometry of $\iota(X)$ and the size of W4. It is not universal in $n$. Residual ambiguity is an orthogonal change of basis of $\mathcal{V}$ preserving $\Phi$, a compact subgroup of $O(\dim\mathcal{V})$.
\end{remark}

\section{Metric and Weight}
\begin{lemma} Assume Lemma~\ref{lma-1} and W4. There is a unique Riemannian metric $g$ on $X$ such that
$$\Gamma(u,v)=g^{-1}(du,dv)\qquad\text{for all }u,v\in\mathcal{V}.$$
This $g$ is the metric whose cometric is the principal symbol of $P$. If the remainder in Lemma 1 is $O(K\varepsilon)$, then
$$\bigl\lVert g-g_{\mathrm{true}}\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
\le C(P,\Lambda,\Lambda',k)\,K\,\varepsilon$$
for every $k$.
\label{lma-3}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}W4 says $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ spans $T^*X$. The assignment $(du,dv)\mapsto\Gamma(u,v)$ is well-defined on that spanning set: $\Gamma(u,u)=\sigma_P(du,du)$, so $du=0$ implies $\Gamma(u,\cdot)=0$. Positive-definiteness is the assumption that the principal symbol of $P$ is an inner product. Dualizing gives $g$.
In a local frame $\{u_a\}\subset\mathcal{V}$ of rank $n=\dim X$,
$$g^{ab}=\Gamma(u_a,u_b)$$
on $\operatorname{span}\{du_a\}$, and $g$ is the inverse matrix. Independence of the frame is change of basis for a cometric. The $C^k$ estimate is Lemma~\ref{lma-1} plus the $C^\infty$ boundedness of a window basis in $C^{k+2}$ on a closed Witten pair.0
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}Let $g$ be the metric of Lemma~\ref{lma-3} and let $\mu$ be the measure determined by
$$\int uv\,d\mu=\Phi(1,u,v),\qquad u,v\in\mathcal{V},$$
extended by continuity to $L^2(\mu)$. Then $\mu$ is a smooth positive multiple of $\mathrm{vol}_g$. The function
$$\alpha=-\log\frac{d\mu}{d\mathrm{vol}_g}$$
is smooth, unique up to an additive constant, and the given operator $P$ is the Witten operator of $(g,\alpha)$. If $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_*$,
$$\bigl\lVert\alpha-\alpha_{\mathrm{true}}-c\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
\le C(P,\Lambda,\Lambda',k)\,K\,\varepsilon.$$
\label{lma-4}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} By the standing category, $P$ is already the Witten operator of some pair $(g_0,\alpha_0)$. Lemma~\ref{lma-3} identifies $g=g_0$, because both metrics have the same principal symbol. The inner product that makes $P$ self-adjoint is $\mu=e^{-\alpha_0}\mathrm{vol}_{g_0}$. On the other hand $\Phi(1,\cdot,\cdot)$ is that inner product, since $1=\ker P$ and $\Phi(1,u,v)=\langle u,v\rangle_{L^2(\mu)}$. Thus $\mu=e^{-\alpha_0}\mathrm{vol}_g$, so $\alpha=\alpha_0+c$. A Witten operator on a closed manifold is determined by $(g,\alpha)$ up to that constant. The $C^k$ estimate follows from Lemma~\ref{lma-3} and the elliptic regularity of $\log(d\mu/d\mathrm{vol}_g)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark} Lemma~\ref{lma-4} is recognition, not construction: it uses that $P$ was Witten. The two measures $\mu$ and $\mathrm{vol}_g$ are the only source of $\alpha$. The packet does not store $\alpha$ separately.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}Combining Lemmas, the packet determines a Riemannian manifold $(\Sigma,g_\Sigma)$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$ and a weight $\alpha_\Sigma$ on $\Sigma$. The evaluation map of Lemma~\ref{lma-2} pulls these back to $(g,\alpha)$ exactly when $\varepsilon=0$, and to within $O(K\varepsilon)$ in $C^k$ when $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_*$.
\end{corollary}


\section{Recognition}
The lemmas of §§3–5 assemble into Theorem~\ref{thm-1} and Theorem~\ref{thm-2}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-1}] Assume $\varepsilon=0$. Lemma 5 gives $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ as a function of $(P,\Phi)$, with remainder zero. Lemma 9 identifies $X$ with the character space $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$ of the finite algebra $(\mathcal{V},m)$ by a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism $\iota$. Lemma 12 dualizes $\Gamma$ to a Riemannian metric $g_\Sigma$ on $\Sigma$ with $\iota^*g_\Sigma=g$. Lemma 13 reads $\alpha$ from $\mu/\mathrm{vol}_g$, up to an additive constant, and identifies $P$ with the Witten operator of that pair. Thus $\iota$ is a $C^\infty$ Riemannian isometry, and the packet determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to that isometry and the constant on $\alpha$. Residual freedom is an orthogonal change of basis of $\mathcal{V}$ preserving $\Phi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-2}] Assume $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_*$, with $\varepsilon_*$ as in Lemma 9. Lemma 5 gives $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ up to an $L^2$ error of size $K\varepsilon$. Lemma 9 produces a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\iota:X\to\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$ of defect $O(\varepsilon)$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$. Lemma 12 upgrades the cometric error to
$$\bigl\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\bigr\rVert_{C^k}\le C(P,\Lambda,\Lambda',k)\,K\,\varepsilon.$$
Lemma 13 gives the same bound for $\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c$. Hence $\iota$ is a $C^k$ almost-isometry of defect $O(K\varepsilon)$, not an isometry. The constant $C$ depends on the given pair, not only on $n$.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
Two packets for the same operator at different admissible cutoffs produce isometric copies if both have $\varepsilon=0$, and almost-isometric copies of defect controlled by the two values of $K\varepsilon$ otherwise. The theorems do not identify merely isospectral Witten operators.
\end{remark}
\begin{remark} A packet-computable modulus, after Lemma 9, is the pointwise remainder $R$ of Lemma 5 evaluated on $\Sigma$. The product $K\varepsilon$ is an a priori stand-in for $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$. Curvature bounds that make $K$ explicit are recorded in Remark 7; they are not used here.
\end{remark}

